          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predicting What Comes Next: Exploring Sequences and Progressions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "175"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.887,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As you already know, in the sequence of natural numbers, every number (or term) is one more than the previous number. In the sequence consisting of all the odd numbers, there is a difference of 2 between any two consecutive terms. In the sequence of triangular numbers, the difference between consecutive terms (among the first six terms) are 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. We may rewrite the triangular numbers in the form of sums of natural numbers as 1 = 1, 3 = 1 + 2, 6 = 1 + 2 + 3, 10 = 1 + 2 "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "+ 3 + 4 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so on. Thus, each term of the triangular number sequence is the sum of the natural numbers up to that term. For example, 15, the fifth triangular number, is equal to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1+2+3+4+5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . This is represented by the diagram in Fig. 8.1, where each triangular number is represented by a triangular array of dots. Can you draw the patterns for the next two terms of the sequence?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.36,
            0.835,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.17,
                0.36,
                0.835,
                0.559
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_1_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.315,
                0.565,
                0.683,
                0.583
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.1: The first five triangular numbers"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.593,
            0.886,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us now shift our attention to the sequence of square numbers, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, ... Note that the differences between consecutive terms (for the first six terms) are 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11. Also "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 = 1, 4 = 1 + 3, 9 = 1 + 3 + 5, 16 = 1 + 3 + 5 + 7,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and so on. Each term in the square number sequence is the sum of the odd numbers up to that term."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.717,
            0.571,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This interesting relationship between the odd numbers and square numbers can be represented by the diagram in Fig. 8.2. Can you explain the relationship? You may recall some of these ideas from Grade 6, Chapter 1!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
